      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 1600 mg four times daily is 6,400 mg/day, which is twice the maximum daily dose of ibuprofen. The usual maximum for an adult is 3,200 mg/day for prescription use, and 1,200 mg/day for OTC use. A single 1,600 mg dose is also double the usual maximum single dose of 800 mg. At this dose the risks of GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular events go up a lot, and there is no added analgesic or anti-inflammatory benefit.\n\nHere are some options that fit a 70 kg adult with normal kidney and liver function and no allergies:\n- **Ibuprofen 800 mg PO three times daily:** 2,400 mg/day. This is a common regimen for inflammatory conditions or moderate pain.\n- **Ibuprofen 800 mg PO four times daily:** 3,200 mg/day. This is the maximum, and it should be used only if needed, for the shortest time possible.\n- **Ibuprofen 400\u2013600 mg PO every 6 hours:** This is typical for mild to moderate pain.\n\nDid you mean one of these, or was 1600 mg meant to be the total daily dose? For example, 400 mg four times daily is 1,600 mg/day. Tell me the dose and frequency you want, and I'll enter it. If you do intend the original dose, please confirm that and give your clinical rationale. I would still suggest checking with pharmacy before it's entered."
      }
     ],
